Use Option B. The only file to change is src/app/reveal.tsx; globals.css stays as it is. Replace lines 63-65 (the "Runs when the page is left or hidden" comment and `return () => observer.disconnect();`) with:

    // Keyboard focus can land on a hidden item without bringing it past the
    // observer's line: the browser scrolls a focused element only far enough
    // to fit it on screen, so it can stop in the bottom 8%. Show the hidden
    // item, and any hidden item around it, as soon as focus moves inside.
    const showFocused = (event: FocusEvent) => {
      if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;
      for (
        let el = event.target.closest<HTMLElement>('[data-reveal-state="hidden"]');
        el;
        el = el.parentElement?.closest<HTMLElement>('[data-reveal-state="hidden"]') ?? null
      ) {
        el.dataset.revealState = "shown";
        observer.unobserve(el);
      }
    };
    document.addEventListener("focusin", showFocused);

    // Runs when the page is left or hidden. Anything still hidden is watched
    // again when the page comes back; what's shown stays shown.
    return () => {
      observer.disconnect();
      document.removeEventListener("focusin", showFocused);
    };

Optionally, in the comment at lines 22-24, change "then shows each one, once, as it scrolls into view" to "then shows each one, once, as it scrolls into view or as keyboard focus moves into it".

Notes:
- --reveal-i is not set on this path, so var(--reveal-i, 0) gives no delay and the normal 0.6s fade runs.
- No reveal target sits inside another today: nested data-reveal-children containers are excluded from TARGETS, and the .prose inside Callout/Try/SiteStory has no data-reveal-children. A plain closest() would therefore be enough. The ancestor loop is cheap insurance, and it is the version I tested.
- The listener is added inside the motion-allowed branch and removed in the cleanup, so reduced motion and hidden pages are unaffected.

A CSS-only alternative is to change the selector at globals.css:3819 to [data-reveal-state="hidden"]:not(:focus-within). It also makes the focused element visible, but the block appears without the fade and disappears again once focus leaves without a scroll.
